<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#honorary-citizenship">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Honorary citizenship</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ehrenbürgerschaft</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Honorary citizenship is a status that a city or other government bestows on a foreign or native individual it considers especially admirable or worthy of the distinction. It is usually symbolic and changes no one's citizenship or nationality, though in Ireland it gives full legal citizenship. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, by contrast, honorary citizens formed a social estate (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosloviye">sosloviye</a>), a privileged subcategory of the estate of urban dwellers. In German municipalities it is the highest distinction a town can award and outlasts the honoree's death. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> has granted it to only eight people since 1963 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> to seven since 1985, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-marino">San Marino</a> offered it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> in 1861.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The way honorary citizenship is granted differs from country to country. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> it needs unanimous approval in both houses of Parliament, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> an act of Congress and the assent of the president. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> it is conferred by the Governor-General. In Ireland honorary citizenship given to a foreigner provides full legal citizenship, including the right to reside and to vote.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s Nationality Act lets the Federal Minister of the Interior naturalise any foreigner without further requirements where there are ties to Germany that justify it, and the Greek Nationality Code offers honorary naturalisation, by presidential decree, to foreigners who have rendered special services to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>German municipal honorary citizenship, though perpetual, can be revoked by the council or parliament of the city, town or state in exceptional cases. For war criminals all such honours were taken away by a directive of the Allied Control Council for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 12 October 1946. Israel may confer honorary citizenship through Yad Vashem on members of the Righteous Among the Nations.</p><p>On 29 March 1861 the Captains Regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-marino">San Marino</a> offered honorary citizenship to the president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>. He replied on 7 May that although the dominion was small, the state was nevertheless one of the most honoured in all history, and this began diplomatic relations between the two.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> awarded it in 1962 to Lucius D. Clay for his role as military governor and in the Berlin airlift, and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a> for his part in the change in world politics that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-the-berlin-wall">fall of the Berlin Wall</a> and the reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In 2002 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> gave it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> football coach Guus Hiddink after the national team reached the semi-finals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">World Cup</a>.</p><p>For the fiftieth anniversary of the first ascent of Everest the Nepalese government gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-hillary">Edmund Hillary</a>, the first foreign national so honoured. In April 2013 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raoul-wallenberg">Raoul Wallenberg</a> became the first person made an honorary citizen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, and in 2017 Malala Yousafzai was made an honorary citizen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Students of the College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> are invited by the mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, at the end of their studies, to become honorary citizens of the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Honorary citizenship” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
